    <div class="container guide-body">
      <h2>Contributing to the Lab</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Entirely optional and off until you turn it on. Contributing sends
        aggregated shot data — never your name, never your files — so the
        Community dashboard and the <a href="/lab">open dataset</a> have real
        amateur numbers behind them instead of thin published averages.
      </p>

      <h3>Data-quality tiers</h3>
      <p class="text-muted">
        A contribution carries a tier describing how much its numbers can be
        trusted. Where the launch monitor GSPro actually reported matches the
        one you told the app you're using, the contribution reaches the
        <strong> Verified</strong> tier; where nothing can be corroborated it
        stays unverified. Spin is only ever published from instruments that
        genuinely measure it rather than estimate it. None of this affects
        whether your data is accepted — it affects how it's weighted.
      </p>

      <h3>What you can set once</h3>
      <p class="text-muted">
        A public display name, a handicap band, an age band, your launch
        monitor, the ball you play, and your bag's brands and models. All
        optional, all stored locally, and all remembered — you fill them in
        once and every later contribution carries them rather than starting
        from a blank dropdown.
      </p>

      <h3>Your contributor ID</h3>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Separately from all of that, the app holds one permanent contributor
        ID: a random value that identifies nothing about you and never
        changes, not even if you rename yourself or reinstall. It's what lets
        the Lab recognise that two contributions came from the same golfer, so
        sending more data updates your numbers instead of adding a second
        version of you to the community averages. Change your display name as
        often as you like — the ID underneath stays put.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Still reading?</h3>
        <p class="text-muted">
